
export anchor KeepTheCardInHandInView2:
    userPrompt:
        \\\
        The spec keeps a moved, focused card in view, as
        spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.motion and
        spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.keyboard say. It says nothing yet about
        the other ways a card can go out of sight, so this is new. Once it's done:
        - When the side panel opens and the board narrows, as
          spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.layout says, the board scrolls
          sideways, in the same quick motion, just enough to keep the open story's
          card fully in view, as long as it was in view before.
        - A story opened from anywhere outside the board, such as a proposal's
          "Added to Backlog" or "Added to <story>" link, or a palette or ribbon
          action that opens one, scrolls the board sideways and its column up or
          down so its card is fully in view, then highlights it as the open story.
        - Clicking a card that is only partly in view opens it and scrolls just
          enough to show it all.
        - In a column scrolled away from its top, cards above the view that are
          added, deleted, or change height (a story's status or step lines
          arriving in Current sprint, or a sprint closing) never move the cards in
          view. The column's scroll shifts by the same amount instead, and the
          thumb moves without jumping, as
          spec/ui/components/scrollbar/index.pi#Scrollbar.steadiness says.
        Write this into Planning's motion section first, then make the board match.
        \\\

    mode: code

    newConversation: true

    systemPrompt:
        \\\
        We're working on the code, located in ./src. Change the code so it does what the prompt asks, as the spec describes it.

        When the work is about Suspense, its modes, its prompts, or its questions, or you are to write a prompt or a question for the user, first read .suspense/suspense-fluency.md once in this conversation, unless you already have: it is how Suspense works.

        The spec's Piton source is in ./spec. Don't change it, and don't read it to learn what the spec says: read the compiled reference instead, as above. If the prompt asks for something the spec doesn't describe, or describes differently, say so in your reply rather than working around it.

        Once you have read what the change depends on, and before your first change, write the constraints this task must meet to ${UNDERSTANDING_FILE}, once. Rewrite it only when a constraint in it turns out to be wrong, never after each page you read. It is a Markdown list and nothing else: one line per constraint, at most twelve, each a single short, specific statement of what must be true, written as a link to the compiled reference file it comes from, such as `- [The chain tab has no bottom border in combined mode](.claude/reference/scope/prompt-mode/chat-input/index.md#chain)`. Only list what the spec says; leave out anything you are deciding yourself. Replace a constraint when you learn it was wrong rather than adding another.

        End with a reply of a few sentences saying what changed, or for a question, the answer, not a recap of the work or of what you read.

        This prompt is the second step of a chain. Its first step changed the spec to meet it, and the spec has been built since. Now change the code so it does what the spec now says, without changing the spec. Follow the spec the prompt references, and the anchors the spec step names below. Where the spec and the prompt disagree, follow the spec, and say so in your reply.

        The code is located in ./src.

        The spec's source, which this step doesn't change, is in ./spec.

        The prompt the chain was sent:

        ${SPEC_PROMPT}

        What the spec step said it changed, its final output:

        ${SPEC_RESULT}
        \\\

    codeTask:
        prompt:
            \\\
            The spec keeps a moved, focused card in view, as
            spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.motion and
            spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.keyboard say. It says nothing yet about
            the other ways a card can go out of sight, so this is new. Once it's done:
            - When the side panel opens and the board narrows, as
              spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.layout says, the board scrolls
              sideways, in the same quick motion, just enough to keep the open story's
              card fully in view, as long as it was in view before.
            - A story opened from anywhere outside the board, such as a proposal's
              "Added to Backlog" or "Added to <story>" link, or a palette or ribbon
              action that opens one, scrolls the board sideways and its column up or
              down so its card is fully in view, then highlights it as the open story.
            - Clicking a card that is only partly in view opens it and scrolls just
              enough to show it all.
            - In a column scrolled away from its top, cards above the view that are
              added, deleted, or change height (a story's status or step lines
              arriving in Current sprint, or a sprint closing) never move the cards in
              view. The column's scroll shifts by the same amount instead, and the
              thumb moves without jumping, as
              spec/ui/components/scrollbar/index.pi#Scrollbar.steadiness says.
            Write this into Planning's motion section first, then make the board match.
            \\\
        result:
            \\\
            I added two paragraphs to Planning's motion section in `spec/scope/planning/index.pi`. `piton check` reports no problems, the build succeeded, and the rebuilt reference shows both paragraphs.

            - **Keeping the open card in view:** if the open story's card was fully in view, the board now scrolls sideways as the side panel opens, in the same 150 ms motion, so the card stays fully in view. A card that was already partly hidden is left alone. Opening a story from outside the board (a proposal's "Added to …" link, or a palette or ribbon action) scrolls the board sideways and the card's column up or down until the card is fully in view, then highlights it. Clicking a card that is only partly in view opens it and scrolls just enough to show all of it. A card already fully in view scrolls nothing.
            - **Changes above the view:** in a column scrolled away from its top, a card above the view can be added, deleted, or change height (status or step lines arriving, a sprint closing). The column's scroll then shifts by the same amount instantly, so the visible cards don't move. The thumb stays steady, linking to `Scrollbar.steadiness`. A column at its top keeps its top.

            **Added by me:** the prompt said "just enough", and I made that explicit: a scroll stops with the card at the nearest edge of the view, never centred. The scroll shift for changes above the view is instant, with no animation.

            **Also fixed:** a line in the existing motion text began with `style:`, which split the section under a stray "Style" heading. I reworded it, so the section is one piece again.

            **Anchors for the code:** `Planning.motion` (the two new paragraphs) and `Scrollbar.steadiness`, with `Planning.layout` for how the side panel narrows the board.
            \\\

    sentFrom: KeepTheCardInHandInView

    model: opus
